# AUTHOR LINE GROUPING — a comment CLAIMED BY AN ELEMENT, in both positions.
#
# MUST EXCLUDE from any bulk `gg fmt` sweep: this fixture is a deliberately
# UNFORMATTED INPUT — the assertion is on what `gg fmt` turns it INTO
# (`fmt_author_rows_comment_claimed_by_an_element`, exact text). A sweep would
# rewrite it into its own expected output and the cell would go green-vacuous.
#
# AXIS = WHO OWNS a comment between two elements. Its siblings put a comment
# where nobody claims it — `author_paren_holds_a_comment.gg` (unclaimed inside a
# grouping paren) and `nested_comment_at_child_close.gg` (unclaimed at a child's
# close). Here an element CLAIMS it, in the two ways an element can:
#
#   `claimed_in_child`  the comment opens the grouping paren's element, so it is
#                       claimed INSIDE a child region. The outer row still breaks
#                       — the comment lies between `10`'s span-end and the
#                       wrapped node's start, which is exactly what the probe
#                       reads — so `10` stands alone and `(a + b), 20` share the
#                       next row.
#   `claimed_mid_elem`  the comment interrupts `a + b` between its own operands,
#                       so it is claimed by the element it sits inside. It is NOT
#                       a break between `a + b` and `20`, and those two keep
#                       their row.
#
# ⚠ WHAT IT PINS, and it is the RATCHET as much as the formatter. The corpus
# guard has no AST, so it tracks comments by frame. Tracking only the UNCLAIMED
# ones made both shapes above false-positive on legal code while `gg fmt`
# honoured the author exactly: a comment claimed inside a child never propagated,
# so the site did not abstain and reported `[[3, 2]]` lost; and a comment claimed
# mid-element was never cleared, so it leaked onto the NEXT element and reported
# `[[1, 1, 2]]` lost. ABSTENTION is about whether a comment EXISTS below a site,
# never about who ended up owning it — and a comment inside an element belongs to
# that element.
#
# RED-verified (Core #12) against the pre-remedy build, quoted as the ratchet
# actually prints it for THIS file as committed:
#
#   tests/fixtures/fmt_author_rows/comment_claimed_by_an_element.gg:
#     2 author row signature(s) lost: [[3, 2], [1, 1, 2]]
#
# — the first signature is `claimed_in_child`, the second `claimed_mid_elem`,
# both with `gg fmt` destroying nothing.
from std.collections import Vector

void main():
    int a = 1
    int b = 2
    Vector[int] claimed_in_child = [10, (# c
 a + b), 20,
        30, 40,]
    Vector[int] claimed_mid_elem = [a + # c
 b, 20,
        30, 40,]
    print(claimed_in_child.len() + claimed_mid_elem.len())
